    <string name="about_link_all">all client traffic</string>
    <string name="about_test_net">Test network</string>
    <string name="about_test_net_sub">TUN · sharing upstream</string>
    <string name="about_test_net_note">drop → packets have nowhere to go</string>
    <string name="about_xray_sub">VPN client</string>
    <string name="about_link_reality">encrypted channel over the phone’s regular network</string>
    <string name="about_server">Your VPN server</string>
    <string name="about_server_sub">exit to the internet</string>
    <string name="about_noroot_title">Why no root</string>
    <string name="about_noroot_text">Android lets only system processes share traffic through their own tunnel. RouterSpot connects to the phone’s own wireless debugging (like a computer over ADB) and starts a service with shell rights. The service creates a test network and asks the system to make it the hotspot upstream.</string>
    <string name="about_leak_title">Leak protection</string>
    <string name="about_leak_text">• The hotspot upstream is the tunnel only. If the VPN drops, packets have nowhere to go: clients are offline, but never direct.\n• A guard in the service and in the app: if the system switches sharing to mobile data or the service dies, all sharing turns off, including USB and Bluetooth tethering turned on by hand.\n• Xray restarts automatically; after 5 failures in a row sharing stops.\n• Clients get no IPv6.</string>
    <string name="about_limits_title">Limitations</string>
    <string name="about_limits_text">• Android 13+. Hidden APIs depend on the firmware — on some phones the mechanism may not work.\n• The service lives until a restart. To start it again you need wireless debugging, which needs a connection to any Wi-Fi network.\n• On phones that can’t run Wi-Fi and a hotspot at the same time, debugging turns off when sharing starts — the service keeps running.</string>
